## Proof

Invert about a circle centered at $A$. Denote images with primes: $B\mapsto B'$, $C\mapsto C'$, $D\mapsto D'$. Since $B,C,D\neq A$, all three images are finite points.

Under this inversion:
- A (genuine) circle through $A$ and $B$ maps to a line through $B'$ not passing through $A$ (the center). Hence the pencil of circles through $A,B$ maps to the family $\mathcal L$ of all lines through $B'$, **except** the single line $AB'$ (which is the image of the degenerate "line $AB$," not a genuine circle).
- A (genuine) circle through $C$ and $D$ maps to a circle through $C'$ and $D'$. Hence the pencil of circles through $C,D$ maps to the family $\mathcal F$ of all circles through $C',D'$; if $A,C,D$ are not collinear, $\mathcal F$ also contains the line $C'D'$ (the image of the genuine circle through $A,C,D$).

The hypothesis becomes:

> Every line in $\mathcal L$ meets every member of $\mathcal F$.

**Lemma.** *For distinct points $C',D'$, the intersection of the closed disks of all circles through $C',D'$ is the closed segment $C'D'$.*

*Proof of Lemma.* Set coordinates with the midpoint of $C'D'$ at the origin, $C'=(-a,0)$, $D'=(a,0)$. Every circle through $C',D'$ has center $(0,h)$ on the perpendicular bisector and radius $r=\sqrt{a^{2}+h^{2}}$ for some $h\in\mathbb R$. A point $X=(x,y)$ lies in its closed disk iff
$$x^{2}+(y-h)^{2}\le a^{2}+h^{2}\iff x^{2}+y^{2}-a^{2}\le 2yh.$$
For this to hold for **all** $h\in\mathbb R$: if $y>0$, sending $h\to-\infty$ drives the right side to $-\infty$ (violation); if $y<0$, sending $h\to+\infty$ does the same. Thus $y=0$, and the inequality reduces to $x^{2}\le a^{2}$, i.e. $|x|\le a$ — precisely the segment $C'D'$. ∎

**Applying the hypothesis.** Fix any circle $\gamma\in\mathcal F$ through $C',D'$. If $B'$ lay strictly outside $\gamma$, the lines through $B'$ disjoint from $\gamma$ would form an open cone of directions (the complement of the angle subtended by $\gamma$ at $B'$, bounded by the two tangents) — infinitely many lines. Removing the single line $AB'$ from $\mathcal L$ still leaves a line through $B'$ disjoint from $\gamma$, contradicting the hypothesis. Hence $B'$ is inside or on every circle $\gamma\in\mathcal F$:
$$B'\in\bigcap_{\gamma\in\mathcal F}\overline{\operatorname{disk}(\gamma)}=\text{segment }C'D',$$
by the Lemma. In particular $B'$ lies on the line $C'D'$. (When $A,C,D$ are not collinear, the line member $C'D'\in\mathcal F$ separately forces the same conclusion: every line in $\mathcal L$ meets the line $C'D'$ iff $B'\in C'D'$.)

**Concluding.** Two cases:

- **$A,C,D$ collinear.** Then $C',D'$ lie on the line through $A$ (the inversion center). The line $C'D'$ passes through $A$ and inverts to itself. Since $B'\in C'D'$, we obtain $B\in C'D'=\text{line }ACD$. Hence $A,B,C,D$ are collinear.

- **$A,C,D$ not collinear.** The line $C'D'$ does not pass through $A$, so it inverts back to the circle through $A,C,D$. Since $B'\in C'D'$, the point $B$ lies on this circle. Hence $A,B,C,D$ are cocyclic.

In both cases $A,B,C,D$ are either collinear or cocyclic. $\blacksquare$

